Ability quintile (Q1--2 vs.\ Q4--5) & ${\sim}$2,600 / ${\sim}$2,600 & 2.9 & 0.027 & 1.19 \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ 3) & 161 / 308 & 61.3 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2023 (sanity) & 540 / 540 & 0.18 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2024 (sanity) & ${\sim}$400 / ${\sim}$400 & 1.3 & -- & -- \\
Within-subject (avg, 5 subj.) & \textit{varies} & 38.1 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

\looseness=-1
Direct comparison of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} on 9,229 annotated items confirms that the two methods capture orthogonal signals. The enrichment ratio is 0.964 (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): DIF-C rates are 30.7\% among web-contaminated items and 31.8\% among clean items. Association tests confirm near-independence ($\phi = 0.012$, OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15], Jaccard $= 0.206$). This near-independence holds across contamination subtypes (input-only enrichment $= 0.986$, input-and-label $= 0.960$). The null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). The Li et al.\ labels are the wrong ground truth for a differential method. 
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF is sensitive to differential item behavior when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

\looseness=-1
Two structural controls and a sensitivity check fail to reduce temporal C\% below 31\% (\Cref{tab:structural_comparison}): matched-ability quintiles, within-subject DIF (C\% $= 38.1\%$; cross-subject C\% is more conservative due to lower local dependence), and IRT $\theta$-conditioning ($r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$). Logistic regression on nine subjects (1{,}787 items; 9/57---full-benchmark rates require further analysis) detects an additional 17.7\% with non-uniform DIF ($|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$; effect-size criterion; LR $p$-values not adjusted for clustering); the union reaches 47\% (Jaccard $= 0.28$), confirming that MH-based ${\approx}$27--29\% captures only the uniform component. Ability-matched analysis yields C\% $= 28.9\%$ (temporal) and $28.3\%$ (Llama-2 vs.\ 3)---consistent matched values (${\sim}$28--29\%) despite vastly different unmatched rates (\Cref{app:structural}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Structural controls and sensitivity checks for temporal DIF. None reduces C\% below 31\%; $\theta$-conditioning serves as a sensitivity check confirming total-score adequacy.}
\label{tab:structural_comparison}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Approach & Mechanism & Temp.\ C\% & TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & Outcome \\
\midrule
Baseline (ext.) & Cross-bench.\ total score & $0.314_{\pm.009}$ & $0.834_{\pm.027}$ & Reference \\
Matched-ability & Within-quintile temp.\ DIF & ${\sim}$0.31 & -- & Enrich.\ 0.94--1.04 \\
$\theta$-cond.\ (sens.) & $\theta$ replaces total score & 0.312 & 0.876 & $r(\theta,\mathrm{total}) {=} 0.9986$ \\
Within-subject & Per-subject DIF & -- & -- & C\% $= 38.1\%$ ($>$25\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Framework Validation}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

\looseness=-1
Cross-subject matching achieves $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU subjects (4/5 with $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$, upper-bound estimate under uniform binary flipping; \Cref{sec:injection}), while within-subject matching inflates FPR by ${+}0.186$ on average. The $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0$ guarantee holds for single-subject contamination ($k \leq 5$, $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} < 0.007$). Under broader contamination spanning $k = 10$ subjects (18\% of domains), $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ rises to $0.047 \pm 0.022$; at $k = 20$, to $0.069 \pm 0.018$ (\Cref{tab:multi_subject_injection,app:fig_matching}). Even worst-case multi-subject inflation ($\sim$7~pp) cannot account for the observed 31\% C\% rate, which exceeds the 0.18\% random baseline by ${>}170\times$. Under DEFF-adjusted effective sample sizes, worst-case $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ at $k{=}20$ (0.069) represents up to 50\% of the conservative 13.5\% lower bound, underscoring that cross-subject matching should be used with $k \leq 5$ in practice.

\subsection{Downstream Impact}
\label{sec:downstream}

\looseness=-1
Stable-item ranks are preserved across all six benchmarks ($\rho = 0.997$, $\tau = 0.958$; flip rate $\leq 2.8\%$; \Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}). DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative signal ($z = -8$ to $-49$ vs.\ random removal). Model families show distinctive profiles: Llama-3 derivatives gain ${+}53$ median rank positions, Mistral derivatives lose $35$ (\Cref{app:fig_downstream}). Among the 200 most displaced models (top 3.8\%; full-sample flip rate $= 2.0\%$), 33.6\% of pairwise rankings reverse. Removing C-flagged items disproportionately disadvantages 2024 models (cohort gap $= 96.2$ positions, $40\times$ random; $p < 0.001$).

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF extends across all six METABENCH benchmarks (\Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}): C\% ranges from 17.9\% (HellaSwag~\citep{zellers2019hellaswag}) to 31.3\% (MMLU), with knowledge/reasoning benchmarks consistently higher than procedural ones (C\% $= 26.9$--$31.3\%$ vs.\ $17.9$--$18.9\%$). These differences may partly reflect item difficulty distributions; we treat them as descriptive.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. C\% = ETS~C items ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024 cohorts; $\rho$/$\tau$ = Spearman/Kendall correlation between full and stable-item rankings; Flip = pairwise rank-flip rate; Ratio = C\%/Rand.\ C\%, with baseline varying by benchmark. All six show substantial DIF (45--174$\times$ above random baseline), with knowledge/reasoning benchmarks exhibiting higher C\% than procedural ones.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrl}
\toprule
Benchmark & Items & C\% & $\rho$ & $\tau$ & Flip\% & Rand.\ C\% & Ratio \\
\midrule
MMLU & 12{,}508 & 31.3 & 0.9965 & 0.9600 & 2.00 & 0.18 & 174$\times$ \\
WinoGrande & 1{,}267 & 30.3 & 0.9943 & 0.9432 & 2.84 & 0.32 & 95$\times$ \\
TruthfulQA & 817 & 29.7 & 0.9971 & 0.9586 & 2.07 & 0.37 & 80$\times$ \\
ARC & 1{,}172 & 26.9 & 0.9962 & 0.9538 & 2.31 & 0.60 & 45$\times$ \\
GSM8K & 1{,}319 & 18.9 & 0.9993 & 0.9843 & 0.78 & 0.30 & 63$\times$ \\
HellaSwag & 10{,}042 & 17.9 & 0.9986 & 0.9722 & 1.39 & 0.28 & 64$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Semi-synthetic injection on GSM8K confirms detection power ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.514$; \Cref{app:gsm8k_details}); exploratory GSM1K analysis ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$; \Cref{app:fig_gsm1k}) suggests DIF captures beyond memorization. DIF remains substantial within 6-month windows (C\% $= 34.35\%$).

